[1]//! Deterministic in-process simulation checks for the game's five behaviours
[2]//! (PRD P1..P5).
[3]//!
[4]//! These are the Developer's self-tests, compiled only for `cargo test` and
[5]//! never part of the shipped binary.  They drive the **real** systems declared
[6]//! in [`crate::game`] on a bare `MinimalPlugins` app with a fixed 1/60 s
[7]//! timestep, so the numbers they assert are the same numbers the observation
[8]//! battery samples from the running game through the frozen contract surfaces.
[9]//!
[10]//! The point of each check is a *semantic* claim, not a pixel claim:
[11]//!
[12]//! * a held `move_dir` produces tens of pixels of displacement, and writing `0`
[13]//!   stops the player dead (no residual velocity);
[14]//! * a jump leaves the ground, rises, falls and lands back on the ground inside
[15]//!   a second, and `Grounded.on_ground` is `true` before take-off and `false`
[16]//!   while airborne;
[17]//! * the coin counter goes 0 -> positive by walking, and never decreases;
[18]//! * the win flag goes `false` -> `true` by walking, one-way, at a position the
[19]//!   player actually stands at;
[20]//! * `FrameCounter.frames` advances exactly once per `app.update()`.
[21]
[22]#![cfg(test)]
[23]
[24]use std::time::Duration;
[25]
[26]use bevy::prelude::*;
[27]use bevy::time::TimeUpdateStrategy;
[28]
[29]use crate::contract::{CoinCounter, FrameCounter, Grounded, InputIntent, Player, WinFlag};
[30]use crate::game::{self, GROUND_Y, MOVE_SPEED};
[31]
[32]/// The fixed timestep every observation is taken at: the headless
[33]/// `ScheduleRunnerPlugin` runs at 60 Hz, so this mirrors the real game.
[34]const DT: f64 = 1.0 / 60.0;
[35]
